            <h1>Exercise Name: Seated Dumbbell Lateral Raises</h1>
            <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
This is an isolation exercise focused on building width and shape on the sides of your shoulders.

 Primary Target Muscle: Lateral Deltoids (Side Delts)—highlighted in orange/yellow on the sides of the shoulders in the animation.
 Secondary Muscles: Anterior Deltoids (Front Delts), Trapezius, and Supraspinatus (Rotator Cuff).


</div></div>
<pre>

<h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1>

<u><b><h4>1. Starting Position</h4></b></u>:
 Sit upright on a flat or slightly inclined bench with your back straight and feet flat on the floor.
 Hold a dumbbell in each hand with a neutral grip (palms facing each other) letting them hang by your sides.


<u><b><h4>2. The Execution (Raise)</h4></b></u>:
 Exhale and slowly raise both arms out to the sides in a wide arc until your upper arms are parallel to the floor (shoulder height).
 Maintain a slight bend in your elbows throughout the movement.
 Pause briefly at the top to squeeze the lateral delts.


<u><b><h4>3. The Lowering Phase</h4></b></u>:
 Inhale and slowly lower the dumbbells back down under control to the starting position.




<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Wider V-Taper Look:</b> Directly builds the lateral heads of the shoulder, giving the upper body a broader, wider appearance.
 <b>◉Eliminates Momentum:</b> Sitting down prevents swinging your lower body, ensuring the target shoulder muscles perform all the lifting.
 <b>◉Shoulder Symmetry:</b> Balances out shoulder growth alongside front raises and rear delt flyes.


<h1>Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

| Fitness Level | Sets | Repetitions | Rest Period |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Beginner | 3 Sets | 12 – 15 Reps | 45–60 sec |
| Intermediate / Advanced | 3 – 4 Sets | 12 – 20 Reps | 45–60 sec |

> Pro Tip: Think about pushing the weights outward toward the walls rather than just pulling them up. Keep your shoulders depressed (don't shrug up toward your ears) to isolate the side delts properly.

